artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a scalar Riccati differential equation with continuous T-periodic coefficients,
    x'(t) = A_2(t) x(t)^2 + A_1(t) x(t) + A_0(t),
where A_0, A_1, A_2 : R -> R are continuous and T-periodic for some fixed T > 0. A T-periodic solution is a solution x(t) defined on all of R satisfying x(t+T) = x(t). A limit cycle is an isolated T-periodic solution (isolated in the set of T-periodic solutions). It is classical that such a Riccati equation either has a continuum (one-parameter family) of T-periodic solutions or has at most 2 limit cycles.

Provide effective, verifiable criteria — expressed directly in terms of the coefficient functions A_0(t), A_1(t), A_2(t) — that determine which of the following four mutually exclusive cases occurs:
  (a) the equation possesses a continuum of T-periodic solutions;
  (b) it has exactly 2 limit cycles;
  (c) it has exactly 1 limit cycle;
  (d) it has no limit cycles.

